
  <sn><md># Setting Up phpMyAdmin with Apache and MySQL on Ubuntu

## 1. Check phpMyAdmin Installation

First, we verified that phpMyAdmin was installed:

```bash
dpkg -l | grep phpmyadmin
```

Then we checked its installation directory:

```bash
ls -la /usr/share/phpmyadmin
```

The directory contained files such as:

```text
index.php
autoload.php
config.sample.inc.php
js/
libraries/
themes/
```

This confirmed that phpMyAdmin was installed correctly.

## 2. Find the Apache Configuration

We checked whether Apache already had a phpMyAdmin configuration:

```bash
find /etc/apache2 -iname '*phpmyadmin*' -print
```

Nothing was returned.

However, this command:

```bash
dpkg -L phpmyadmin | grep apache
```

showed:

```text
/etc/phpmyadmin/apache.conf
```

So the phpMyAdmin Apache configuration existed, but Apache was not loading it.

## 3. Create the Apache Configuration Link

We created a symbolic link from phpMyAdmin's configuration to Apache's `conf-available` directory:

```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/phpmyadmin/apache.conf /etc/apache2/conf-available/phpmyadmin.conf
```

This makes the phpMyAdmin Apache configuration available to Apache.

## 4. Enable the phpMyAdmin Configuration

We enabled the configuration:

```bash
sudo a2enconf phpmyadmin
```

Apache reported:

```text
Enabling conf phpmyadmin.
To activate the new configuration, you need to run:
  systemctl reload apache2
```

## 5. Test Apache Configuration

Before reloading Apache, we checked the configuration:

```bash
sudo apache2ctl configtest
```

The result was:

```text
Syntax OK
```

There was also an `AH00558` warning about Apache's `ServerName`. This warning does not prevent phpMyAdmin from working.

## 6. Reload Apache

Finally, we reloaded Apache:

```bash
sudo systemctl reload apache2
```

After this, phpMyAdmin became accessible through:

```text
http://localhost/phpmyadmin
```

## 7. phpMyAdmin Login

When phpMyAdmin opened, it asked for a username and password.

These credentials are **MySQL credentials**, not necessarily the Linux username and password.

We checked the MySQL accounts with:

```bash
sudo mysql
```

and:

```sql
SELECT user, host, plugin FROM mysql.user;
```

If the MySQL `root` account uses:

```text
auth_socket
```

it is generally better not to change the root authentication just for phpMyAdmin.

Instead, a separate MySQL user can be created:

```sql
CREATE USER 'emon'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'YourStrongPassword';

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON *.* TO 'emon'@'localhost'
WITH GRANT OPTION;

FLUSH PRIVILEGES;

EXIT;
```

Then those credentials can be used to log in to phpMyAdmin.

## Final Structure

The important relationship is:

```text
phpMyAdmin
│
├── Application
│   └── /usr/share/phpmyadmin/
│       └── index.php
│
├── Configuration
│   └── /etc/phpmyadmin/apache.conf
│
└── Apache
    │
    ├── conf-available/
    │   └── phpmyadmin.conf
    │
    └── conf-enabled/
        └── phpmyadmin.conf
             │
             └── points to /etc/phpmyadmin/apache.conf
```

The Apache configuration provides the mapping:

```text
http://localhost/phpmyadmin
            │
            ▼
/usr/share/phpmyadmin/
```

## Complete Command Sequence

For a similar Ubuntu installation, the essential commands are:

```bash
ls -la /usr/share/phpmyadmin

dpkg -L phpmyadmin | grep apache

sudo ln -s /etc/phpmyadmin/apache.conf \
    /etc/apache2/conf-available/phpmyadmin.conf

sudo a2enconf phpmyadmin

sudo apache2ctl configtest

sudo systemctl reload apache2
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost/phpmyadmin
```

and log in using a MySQL account.
